Hi Deepak,
Sorry for the late reply! But I did want to share at least the thinking behind the choices so far and some ideas for the future.

So in no particular order:

- To date, our approach with Renjin has been primarily driven by our own needs for specific projects, which for a variety of reasons possible unique to us, have precluded the ability to use native code at all - so JNI simply wasn't an option for us at some key junctures.

- However, I don't think anything we've done so far would preclude calling out to the GNU R interpreter using JNI. Sharing state between the two interpreters - however - would be more complicated, and things like passing a Renjin closure as an argument to a GNU R function would be pretty tricky.

- Personally though, if I had to choose between spending the time on duct-taping together a JNI-based compatibility layer with GNU R using JNI and expanding Renjin's emulation layer and our little C/Fortran to byte code compiler, I'd probably go with the latter: using the packages via GNU R means giving up a lot of the reasons we built Renjin to begin with:
+ Multiple interpreter contexts per process (very useful for web servers!)
+ Transparent, pluggable storage of R data objects- we often expose large database tables as R data.frames- but you would need to bring all of the data into memory in order to use with GNU R packages. (and yes there are great layers like monet.frame or Hadley's work that give you a set of common functions that can operate on external memory data structures, but the big motivation with Renjin was to be able to run any arbitrary R code on external data)
+ Deferred evaluation, lazy data structures
+ Portable binaries!

- Though there are a lot of R packages - they all in principle rely on a common, though large, set of functionality provided by the base package and by the GNU R C API. The work of ensuring strict compatibility with the R base packages, and incrementally expanding the coverage of our emulation layer for the GNU R C API, lifts all boats: work done on these components for one package will improve support for all packages.

- There are some packages that will probably never run on Renjin because of strong dependencies on GNU R's internals - but my experience that these are mainly packages that offer an approach that competes with Renjin, for example, the bigmemory package or Hadley's superhuman work on plyr or dplyr, rather than the statistical packages themselves.

The plm package you inquired about is simply 7,000 lines of pure R code - no reason that this shouldn't run on Renjin if someone takes a few days to either get the two C source files in MASS to compile to byte code or simply port them to Java.

I think the agony of getting Renjin to talk to the package via JNI in any meaningful way would be an order of magnitude larger, and since you'd have few of Renjin's benefits, you'd probably be better off making the connection between Rserve or rJava.

But that's my two cents! If anyone wants to take a swing at a JNI-based interface, pull requests welcome!!

Best,
Alex

